rted by the shell could already make the later protection order impossible; no secret package edge or protection implementation is added by this reservation. The shell’s installation parties and lifecycle authority follow (`dec:observe:first-start-route`) and (`rule:observe:credential-store-contract`), subject to (`open:observe:credential-installation`). On a supported graceful termination request, ready becomes false, new dispatch stops, bounded owned work is cancelled where allowed, resources are released and the process completes once. Repeated termination requests are idempotent and cannot renew the stopping deadline; forced termination has no cleanup guarantee. A measured shell_stop deadline and an independently enforced supervisor escalation bound are required, not guessed. Ordinary restart uses the same checked entry. The operator intends the following process adapter, subject to confirmation or correction by installation rehearsal: after all startup checks complete, the main process sends exactly one ready notification to an endpoint the supervisor names at start. The supervisor keeps the unit starting from process creation until that notification; an exit or expiry of the start bound before it is a failed start with its own recorded result. The repository alone supplies the early-ready control, since the supervisor trusts the notification. The repository owes the measured longest startup-check duration as shell_start, not an assumed default. The supervisor sends SIGTERM to the main process as its supported graceful termination request, offers no reload in place and performs no automatic restart at this stage. After the stop bound it terminates every remaining process of the unit and records a timed-out stop; the repository owes the measured shell_stop duration used to set that bound. These intentions supply the adapter design, not evidence that the installed supervisor implements it; pure lifecycle controls remain independent of the installation rehearsal. 