rough waits and terminal outcome under (`rule:observe:metering`), and no private path, key or dependency error; source-local acquisition/exit census must show the declared empty secret set. Inspection of unit state and read-scoped logs is an operations surface, not a secretly enabled inspect resolver.

The shell command grammar is pn start or pn edition, with the existing help and version forms of (`dec:output:policy`); neither command takes an edition operand or option. Bare pn, extra operands and an edition option, including --edition, are usage refusals with JSON stderr, empty stdout and exit 2. This separates an installation setting from an invocation action and avoids a per-invocation override hidden in an argument list, shell history or process inspection; environment and file delivery do not make this public selection a secret. Parsing help/version keeps the shared runner's terminal semantics, including observation-configuration precedence under (`amend:observe:entry-origin`). Both commands use that origin route: capture is the first controlled statement of main, then command parsing and public selection occur in its synchronous preparation before any worker; capture itself reads no environment or file. A parse terminal performs no edition acquisition. For a parsed command, source presence is checked before either edition value or file contents, then the sole supplied source is read and its syntax checked, then the reserved-input absence is checked; start additionally validates its notification input before any lifecycle work. Every preparation refusal is retained for the shared runner to install observation only after the decision. No command or dependency worker, executor dispatch, store, listener or ready notification is started by edition; observation of the check itself still follows the shared runner.

PN_EDITION contains exactly the textual schema/register coordinate pair of (`amend:api:test-schema-plan`). PN_EDITION_FILE contains the explicit absolute path of the sole public edition file; the installation or supervisor fixes this path in the process environment, with no command-line path, working-directory lookup, home-directory lookup, compiled path, search or fallback. The location and contents are public inputs under (`rule:storage:configuration`), and an installation must not put a private path or secret in either setting. An absent PN_EDITION_FILE means no file source is designated; the executable does not search for an ambient file. A set source selector counts as supplied even if its value is empty: presence of PN_EDITION and PN_EDITION_FILE together refuses before opening a file, even if their intended editions agree or the designated file is missing, because precedence could let a stale setting silently override the intended one. Neither set refuses without a default, because a default would start an edition that was never selected. A sole empty, non-text or malformed PN_EDITION, or empty, non-text, NUL-bearing, non-absolute or unusable PN_EDITION_FILE, is a usage refusal; setting the file selector commits to that source rather than silently falling back when it fails. An unusable file path is one the platform cannot represent or open for reading, or whose target cannot be read to the required bounded EOF as a regular file; these failures are usage refusals, not absence or a support verdict. The opened file must be a regular file and consists of exactly the same coordinate text followed by one LF, without a BOM, whitespace, comments or additional keys. An empty, unreadable, missing or malformed selected file refuses as usage with no start. Version components have the existing domain of (`sig:xchg:version-api`); each uses unsigned decimal with no leading zero except zero itself, and checked conversion refuses overflow. This grammar and the fixed coordinate prefixes give a finite maximum input length, so reading uses at most that length plus one excess-byte probe and requires EOF, never an unbounded read or parser allocation. The environment form has no final LF. This single-line public input is chosen over another policy parser because it carries only a selection; the descriptor is read once and closed, with no reload or second read at start. No claim of immutable installation files or authenticated filesystem ownership is made by this public reader.

A syntactically valid but unembedded coordinate pair is an unsupported selection, not malformed input and not a usage error. With selection outside the argument list, pn edition provides a check that reveals the selection without starting anything. It writes one JSON object with members edition, source and supported, in that order, followed by LF: edition is the exact selected coordinate text, source is environment or file, and supported is a Boolean stating membership in the build's embedded edition roster. The embedded edition roster contains exactly the schema/register pairs for which this build embeds both generated artifacts at their assigned coordinates; an unserved register draft at no assigned coordinate is not a roster member. The reserved first control pair therefore reports supported=false until its initial register has been generated and both artifacts are embedded at that pair; this inspection neither assigns the coordinate nor admits a measurement. Membership is not a fresh validation of limits or a promise that pn start will start: construction and runtime prerequisites can still refuse, and a pending register does not prevent this public check. The edition command's help states this distinction and that, as a result command under (`dec:output:policy`), it refuses terminal stdout with usage exit 2 and must be piped or redirected. A valid selection returns exit 0 even when supported is false; a selection refusal emits only its JSON usage diagnostic and exit 2, and output failure is runtime failure 1 under (`dec:output:policy`), with the existing partial-write limitation. It reports no file path or input bytes beyond the public edition. For start, a syntactically valid unsupported selection retains the existing EditionUnsupported meaning when start is dispatched; no preflight check fabricates a start result, and construction's LimitsUnadmitted refusal still precedes dispatch under (`amend:api:test-schema-plan`). This selection contract changes neither that ordering nor the executor's failure vocabulary. Selection names an embedded edition, not an artifact location: no file supplies the reviewed schema, register, finite ceilings or an alternate policy, and no default edition is selected on refusal.

The reserved supervisor-only capability has the public name pn-connection-input; its eventual delivery form is left to C9's design and admission rather than inferred from the shell's absence check. The descriptor advertisement is LISTEN_FDNAMES, the public colon-separated name roster accompanying inherited descriptors through LISTEN_PID and LISTEN_FDS. Detection reads only that public metadata, never descriptor contents or a descriptor's target path: the reserved name anywhere in the roster, any other advertised service descriptor or malformed metadata refuses as usage before descriptor acquisition, because this shell has no service-descriptor role. No activation metadata is the ordinary absent case; an explicitly empty roster with a zero count is also absent only with a valid LISTEN_PID for this process. Positive counts, mismatched process identity, omitted companion metadata or malformed names never turn into absence. Presence of LISTEN_PIDFDID, including an empty value, refuses as unsupported supervisor metadata with usage exit 2; this edition does not validate that optional process-identity convention and never treats it as permission to ignore or adopt descriptors. For credential-directory delivery the supervisor advertises this reserved input with the public environment marker PN_CONNECTION_INPUT, whose only specified value is the fixed name pn-connection-input, never a path or connection bytes. Presence of that marker, including an empty or malformed value, refuses as usage without consulting CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY, looking up a path, listing a directory or opening a credential. The marker is needed because CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY supplies a possibly private path, not a public member roster, and the ordinary safe environment getter copies its value even if the caller only asks whether it is present. The shell therefore does not read that variable to claim a public-only presence check. This minimal advertisement detects the supervisor's declared directory delivery of this input, not the existence or contents of a file; a supervisor providing that delivery must supply the marker, and an unadvertised directory delivery cannot be detected by this metadata-only check. Both commands apply both checks. Absence of these advertisements establishes only absence under the supervisor delivery contract, not absence of concealed descriptors or files; installation owns truthful metadata and custody. The pure adapter accepts these public presence facts, and the process-adapter subcut binds their detection. An inherited read descriptor would require C9 to admit a safe descriptor-adoption dependency and its custody boundary: raw descriptor adoption would require unsafe code denied outside the secret home's inventoried sites, while the existing safe process-descriptor route is available only in the secret home, not the node or host. Reusing that route through a new secret-home operation would contradict the selected consumer placement under (`dec:observe:credential-packages`) and (`amend:observe:supervisor-entry`) unless separately reconsidered; this reservation admits neither an edge nor an operation. Credential-directory delivery can instead open a runtime path with ordinary file reading into the unchanged host Configuration::acquire reader, but C9 must decide that acquisition and its protected path handling, with installation and supervisor owning the file's access contract under (`rule:observe:credential-store-contract`). Neither option puts connection bytes in arguments or environment values, and neither is chosen by this shell: the existing host/home separation, secret discipline and installation hold remain unchanged.

The readiness channel is the supervisor's local notification protocol, because the supervisor already implements and trusts that protocol while an inherited write descriptor would require a separate reader it does not offer: the main process sends the single datagram READY=1, without a trailing LF or additional members, to NOTIFY_SOCKET after all startup checks and only while not stopping. This edition admits a nonempty absolute filesystem Unix-domain socket address supplied at runtime, rejects embedded NUL, overlong addresses, relative addresses and abstract-namespace addresses, and checks that the named endpoint is a socket rather than a regular file or a final symlink before attempting notification. The endpoint is a private capability held and diagnosed only through its admitted protected representation, not printed with argv or public selection. These checks establish address form and endpoint type, not supervisor authenticity or immunity to replacement; the trusted supervisor and installation own endpoint access and identity. A send-only unbound local datagram endpoint performs no bind, listen, name resolution or network-family operation. Missing or invalid notification input is a start usage refusal before lifecycle workers; edition neither reads nor requires it. pn start requires this supervisor notification socket and cannot be started by hand outside such a supervisor; its help states that requirement. Failure to send is runtime failure with no ready claim or success completion; a successful send reports enqueueing, not an acknowledgement from the supervisor. There is no inherited readiness write descriptor, retrying ready notification or extra readiness record on stdout. Restricting the address to a filesystem socket keeps the first adapter's validation inspectable; another local address form requires its own admission. The supervisor must accept the main process's notification and enforce the independent startup/stop bounds in installation rehearsal; pure controls prove only the decisions.

SIGTERM alone requests the supported graceful termination. No other signal is intercepted or unblocked as a lifecycle request: the process adapter leaves its runtime or inherited disposition unchanged. A signal with a default terminating disposition ends the process directly, and SIGKILL remains uncatchable. In particular the adapter does not reset the runtime’s SIGPIPE handling, so a broken output pipe can follow the existing fallible-write contract instead of introducing another exit policy; a supervisor-supplied ignored or blocked non-SIGTERM signal is not silently normalized. Signal death promises neither cleanup nor a JSON completion and is reported by the supervisor as process termination, not converted to an invented exit-1 return. A requested orderly termination before the first ready notification returns runtime failure 1 with empty stdout and its public JSON stderr outcome. After ready, only a completed orderly stop returns success 0 and exactly {"edition":"<selected coordinate pair>","outcome":"stopped"} followed by LF, with those two members in that order; stopped is the only outcome value in a success completion. Failed or timed-out stop and output failure return runtime failure, with no successful completion promised, and a failed write retains the shared partial-write limitation. The object has no timing or accounting members because (`rule:observe:metering`) already owns them. This keeps a cancelled startup distinct from a service that became ready and then stopped; stop idempotence, nonrenewable deadlines, notification arbitration and internal cleanup transitions remain the lifecycle design's work, not a second signal policy here.

Focused one-process unit controls for these operator-surface decisions use supplied argument, environment-presence, file-byte, notification and signal facts rather than a server, child process or real signal: edition_both_sources_refuse pins presence conflict even for equal values; edition_neither_source_refuses pins the lack of a default; edition_arguments_refuse pins bare invocation and every argument route to edition selection; edition_reports_selection pins the three output members, roster membership including the ungenerated-register case, unsupported reporting and no start dispatch; edition_input_forms pins the closed file/environment grammar and usage refusals; reserved_input_refuses pins descriptor and directory advertisements, optional identity-metadata refusal and absence cases without content or path acquisition; notification_contract pins endpoint/payload selection and no notification for edition; termination_output_contract pins SIGTERM-only graceful classification and the before/after-ready output split. Their names are proposals for the lifecycle implementation; each receives its own claim under (`rule:testing:site`). The node implementer with repository review owns these focused controls; actual notification, signal and supervisor behavior stays with the process-adapter and installation rows of (`preview:design:first-node-cuts`), with the existing deferred evidence and its owner/time unchanged.

The operator intends the following order, subject to (`open:observe:credential-installation`): generation under (`dec:observe:first-start-route`), the installation's composition of each role's complete connection input, the administrator's verifier-only role provisioning, the unprivileged schema-owner task, then the node; the store service needs no client password. Each stage waits for its predecessor's completed result under (`rule:observe:credential-store-contract`). Ordinary restart reuses the same connection input. Rotation generates again, sets the new verifier on the role, replaces the connection input and restarts the service. The delivered-set publication, marker, authorization, selection and reconciliation order remains only the retained model of (`model:observe:credential-initialization`), conditional on a new decision selecting that protocol. These installation intentions remain subject to rehearsal under (`open:observe:credential-installation`). Start the chain daemon before enabling an edition that requires it; the read-only stage creates no chain credential. Establish secret custody and read public bootstrap and the connection input, validate the edition and every bound, then bind; operational dependency unavailability permits a not-ready listener, but malformed bootstrap, unsupported edition, a refused connection input or an incompatible store identity fails startup. Retry dependency acquisition only within declared bounded backoff and connection budgets. Health remains available while not ready; domain operations return ServiceUnavailable/503 until their required dependencies and recovery checks succeed. Persisted reads may be served only if their declared contract does not require a presently unavailable chain view; readiness never substitutes for per-operation checks. On stop, stop accepting connections and dispatching new domain work, close idle connections, drain owned work under shutdown_drain, then release the runtime sessions. Cancel only where the domain contract permits; recover uncertain commits by their structural identity, never from the HTTP status or a cancelled future. A forced stop may leave uncertainty for startup reconciliation. Stop the node before its services; unexpected service loss makes readiness false, and restored reachability does not make it true while any reconciliation remains pending. Restart, including a scheduled reboot, uses the same start and reconciliation path with the same durable state and connection input; no manual successful-response reconstruction is required.

The shell edition of (`amend:api:node-package`) requires neither service and cannot open either connection; it does not implement the credential route of (`dec:observe:first-start-route`) or the start prerequisites of (`amend:api:health-lifecycle`).
